from lib import *

# ---------------- S2: ring convolution ----------------
S21 = S("S2.1",
  "The synthetic transform $(F_t^-z)_j=\\frac1t\\sum_k\\omega_t^{-jk}z_k$ over $\\mathcal R_r=\\mathbb C[y]/(y^r+1)$ with the root $\\omega_t=y^{2r/t}$ is computed by radix-two decimation-in-frequency rounds with twiddle monomials, leaves its outputs in bit-reversed order $B_t$, and the reverse procedure consumes that order and implements $F_t^+B_t^{-1}$.",
  "Use powers of $y$ as exact roots of unity, since multiplication by $y^a$ is a signed coefficient shift and an isometry; verify character cancellation directly because the ring has zero divisors; build the transform by the butterfly $H_0$ and a twiddle at each of $\\log_2t$ rounds.",
  M("The ring has zero divisors, so character cancellation is verified directly rather than by dividing by $\\omega_t^j-1$.", "explicit",
    "The ring has zero divisors, so we verify character cancellation"),
  [
    I("S2.1.1", "Multiplication by $y^a$ permutes the coefficients and changes some signs, so it is an isometry of the sup norm and every power of $\\omega_t$ is an isometry; hence both normalized transforms are contractions.",
      "We always use representatives of degree less than $r$.  Multiplication"),
    I("S2.1.2", "Character cancellation: $\\sum_{k=0}^{t-1}\\omega_t^{jk}=t$ if $t\\mid j$ and $0$ otherwise; for $j=2^vj_0$ with $j_0$ odd and $v<\\log_2t$, replacing $k$ by $k+t/2^{v+1}$ permutes the summands and multiplies the sum by $y^{rj_0}=-1$, so it vanishes because the additive group has no two-torsion.",
      "Only the second case needs proof.  Write $j=2^v j_0$ with $j_0$ odd"),
    I("S2.1.3", "Hence $F_t^+F_t^-=t^{-1}I$.",
      "and applying this identity proves the final formula."),
    I("S2.1.4", "The decimation-in-frequency step pairs $z_k$ with $z_{k+t/2}$ by $H_0$, multiplies the $b=1$ output by $\\omega_t^{-k}$ and recurses on the two halves, because $(F_t^-z)_{2h+b}=\\frac1{t/2}\\sum_{k<t/2}(\\omega_t^2)^{-hk}\\omega_t^{-bk}\\frac{z_k+(-1)^bz_{k+t/2}}2$.",
      "$F_t^-$ because, for $b\\in\\{0,1\\}$,"),
    I("S2.1.5", "After all $a=\\log_2t$ rounds the named slots from high to low contain the frequency bits from low to high, so the entry $(F_t^-z)_j$ occupies slot $\\operatorname{rev}_a(j)$ and the stored order is the record permutation $B_t$ (bit reversal).",
      "entry $(F_t^-z)_j$ occupies slot $\\operatorname{rev}_a(j)$."),
    I("S2.1.6", "Since $H_0^2=I/2$ the reversed level is half the inverse of the forward level, so the reverse procedure composed with $B_tF_t^-$ is $2^{-a}I=I/t$; as the forward procedure is invertible, the reverse procedure is $F_t^+B_t^{-1}$.",
      "As $H_0^2=I/2$, the reversed level is half the inverse"),
    I("S2.1.7", "For $D$ axes the individual axis steps group into one parallel butterfly $\\mathcal H$ followed by a recordwise monomial $J_E$ multiplying each record by $y^E$, because the twiddle of an axis depends only on that axis's address bits and commutes with the other axes' butterflies.",
      "Consequently the individual axis steps may be grouped as a parallel"),
  ],
  page="synthetic-transform",
  frm=["polynomial-ring-mod", "dft", "multidimensional-dft", "butterfly", "roots-of-unity", "fast-fourier-transform-and-divide-and-conquer", "ring-and-group-notions-for-polynomials"],
  salient="Character cancellation holds in a ring with zero divisors because shifting $k$ by $t/2^{v+1}$ multiplies the sum by $y^{rj_0}=-1$ exactly when $t\\nmid j$; this is where $y^r=-1$ is used.",
  adds=[B("t", "transform length", "The length of one transform axis, a power of two dividing $2r$.", "count"),
        B("\\omega_t", "synthetic root", "The monomial $y^{2r/t}$, which acts as a signed coefficient shift and plays the role of a primitive $t$th root of unity.", "direct"),
        B("B_t", "bit reversal", "The record permutation moving the entry at index $j$ to $\\operatorname{rev}_a(j)$, the order in which the rounds leave the outputs.", "direct")])

S22 = S("S2.2",
  "With $T=rM$ and $d-1$ axes of power-of-two lengths $t_i\\in\\{r/2,r\\}$, the forward synthetic transform with its layout permutation $L$ and the opposite procedure each cost $O(Tp[dK+pK^{\\tau-1}+\\ell d^{\\lambda'}])$ with error less than $\\sqrt2\\,\\ell\\,2^{-p}$.",
  "Move the selected bits of the participating axes to a common offset in consecutive $K$-bit chunks by prefix-slot moves and $O(p/K)$ chunk interchanges, then perform each of the $\\ell$ rounds as one simultaneous layer, with a signed-shift monomial and one truncation per round.",
  M("To apply the simultaneous layer in a transform round the selected bits of the participating axes must sit at a common offset in consecutive $K$-bit chunks, so a layout permutation is needed first and is charged in the cost.", "explicit",
    "we must place the selected bits of the participating axes at a common"),
  [
    I("S2.2.1", "With $b=(\\ell-1)\\bmod K$ and $q=\\lfloor(\\ell-1)/K\\rfloor$, at most $D(b+1)=O(dK)$ listed slots are moved to a prefix, costing $O(TpdK)$, and each axis is left with $q$ consecutive $K$-bit chunks.",
      "listed slots, so the moves cost $O(TpdK)$."),
    I("S2.2.2", "Reordering the chunks from axis-major to chunk-major order uses at most $Dq-1$ exchanges of width-$K$ chunks, each costing $O(TpK^\\tau)$.",
      "with the required chunk if they differ.  This uses at most $Dq-1$"),
    I("S2.2.3", "Hence the exchanges cost $O(Tp\\,DqK^\\tau)=O(Tp\\,pK^{\\tau-1})$, using $Dq=O(p/K)$ because $d\\ell=O(p)$.",
      "=O\\bigl(Tp\\,pK^{\\tau-1}\\bigr)."),
    I("S2.2.4", "Each of the $\\ell$ rounds on the remaining chunks is one application of the simultaneous layer, costing $O(Tpd^{\\lambda'})$ per round and $O(Tp\\ell d^{\\lambda'})$ in total.",
      "$O(Tpd^{\\lambda'})$ per round and $O(Tp\\ell d^{\\lambda'})$ in total."),
    I("S2.2.5", "Rounds on prefix slots process the participating bits individually at width $p+D+O(1)=O(p)$ bits, at most $b+1\\le K$ rounds, giving cost $O(TpdK)$.",
      "most $b+1\\leq K$ rounds on prefix slots, giving cost $O(TpdK)$."),
    I("S2.2.6", "The monomial $y^E$ with $E=er+s$, $0\\le s<r$, is a split of the coefficient list before position $r-s$, a swap of the pieces with negation of the moved tail, and the common sign $(-1)^e$, costing $O(rp)$ per record.",
      "To implement the monomial, write $E=er+s$ with $0\\leq s<r$."),
    I("S2.2.7", "Truncation toward zero is odd and acts separately on each part, so $J_EQ_p(\\mathcal Hz)=Q_p(J_E\\mathcal Hz)$: the actual call sequence equals truncating once after the exact contraction round.",
      "J_EQ_p(\\mathcal H z)=Q_p(J_E\\mathcal H z)."),
    I("S2.2.8", "A contraction does not increase the norm of the difference between computed and exact arrays and each round adds one truncation below $\\sqrt2\\,2^{-p}$, so starting from zero error at most $\\ell$ rounds give error less than $\\sqrt2\\,\\ell\\,2^{-p}$ and disk output, the layout permutations being isometries.",
      "between the computed and exact arrays; the single truncation then"),
    I("S2.2.9", "The exact operators of the forward and opposite procedures are $LBF_{\\boldsymbol t}^-$ and $F_{\\boldsymbol t}^+B^{-1}L^{-1}$, so the entry of frequency address $j$ is stored at $L(Bj)$.",
      "(L\\mathcal D_-L^{-1})L=LB F_{\\boldsymbol t}^-,"),
  ],
  frm=["fast-butterfly-layer", "address-chunk-interchange", "synthetic-transform", "fixed-point-truncation", "polynomial-ring-mod", "multidimensional-dft"],
  salient="The forward output is stored in the permuted layout $LB$ and is never put back in order: the layout and bit-reversal permutations are retained for the pointwise product and consumed by the opposite procedure.",
  adds=[B("\\ell", "log ring degree", "The exponent with $r=2^\\ell$, which is also the number of butterfly rounds on a long axis.", "count")])

S23 = S("S2.3",
  "Two disk-grid polynomials $f,g\\in\\mathcal R_r$ with $p$-bit grid coefficients can be multiplied exactly, returning $Q_p(fg/r)$, in $O(rp\\log(rp))$ time on a fixed number of tapes, using the established multiplier only as a subroutine.",
  "Evaluate the integer coefficient polynomials at $B_0=2^{4p}$ to get signed integers of $4pr$ bits, multiply the four needed pairs with the established multiplier, and read the product coefficients back by centered residues and carries, then reduce modulo $y^r+1$ and truncate.",
  M("This is a signed form of Kronecker substitution, adapted so that negative coefficients and the tape format are handled.", "recoverable"),
  [
    I("S2.3.1", "Writing $f=2^{-p}(a+ib)$ and $g=2^{-p}(c+ie)$ with integer polynomials of coefficients at most $2^p$ in absolute value, one sets $B_0=2^{4p}$ and forms the signed evaluation integers $A_u=u(B_0)$.",
      "Set $B_0=2^{4p}$"),
    I("S2.3.2", "Every coefficient of an ordinary product of two such polynomials satisfies $|h_j|\\le r2^{2p}<2^{3p}<B_0/2$, so after lower coefficients are removed the next one is identified by its centered residue modulo $B_0$.",
      "|h_j|\\leq r2^{2p}<2^{3p}<B_0/2."),
    I("S2.3.3", "Forming $A_u$ block by block with carries $\\gamma_j\\in\\{-1,0\\}$ gives a fixed-width two's-complement encoding with $\\gamma_r=-1$ exactly when $A_u<0$, so the sign is handled in a constant number of scans.",
      "$\\gamma_j\\in\\{-1,0\\}$, the carry stays in $\\{-1,0\\}$.  Telescoping"),
    I("S2.3.4", "Reading a product integer back, with the unique centered representative $\\widehat h_j\\in[-B_0/2,B_0/2)$ of $q_j+\\rho_j$ and a carry $\\rho_{j+1}\\in\\{0,1\\}$, returns exactly $h_j$ by induction on the residual identity, ending with $\\rho_J+\\varepsilon=0$.",
      "integer in $[-B_0/2,B_0/2)$ congruent to $q_j+\\rho_j$ modulo $B_0$,"),
    I("S2.3.5", "The real and imaginary numerator streams are $ac-be$ and $ae+bc$, and reduction modulo $y^r+1$ subtracts the coefficient at $j+r$ from that at $j$.",
      "$ac-be$ and $ae+bc$.  To reduce them modulo $y^r+1$, subtract the"),
    I("S2.3.6", "Each reduced coefficient is a sum of exactly $r$ signed products, so $|H_j|\\le r2^{2p}$ and $\\|fg/r\\|_\\infty\\le\\|f\\|_\\infty\\|g\\|_\\infty$: the normalized product of disk polynomials lies in the disk, which is why the product is divided by $r$.",
      "$\\|fg/r\\|_\\infty\\leq\\|f\\|_\\infty\\|g\\|_\\infty$; in particular the"),
    I("S2.3.7", "Four exact integer products of $O(rp)$ bits dominate the linear packing, extraction, wrapping and truncation passes, so the total cost is $O(rp\\log(rp))$.",
      "Four exact integer products dominate the linear"),
  ],
  page="signed-packing",
  frm=["established-multiplier", "polynomial-ring-mod", "fixed-point-truncation", "polynomials", "ring-and-group-notions-for-polynomials", "binary-representation-and-bit-operations"],
  salient="The negacyclic wrap needs no special treatment: subtracting the coefficient at $j+r$ from the one at $j$ realizes $y^r=-1$, and dividing by $r$ keeps the result in the disk because each coefficient averages $r$ signed products.",
  adds=[B("B_0", "packing base", "The integer $2^{4p}$ at which coefficient polynomials are evaluated to pack them into integers.", "direct")])

S24 = S("S2.4",
  "Normalized convolution $\\mathcal M_r(f,g)=(f*g)/T$ on disk-grid arrays over $\\mathcal R_r$ on $d-1$ axes is approximated with error less than $\\sqrt2\\,M(3\\ell+1)2^{-p}$ in cost $O(Tp[dK+pK^{\\tau-1}+\\ell d^{\\lambda'}+\\log(rp)])$.",
  "Perform both forward synthetic transforms and keep their common $LB$ order, multiply corresponding polynomial records by signed packing divided by $r$, apply the opposite procedure to that order and scale exactly by $M=T/r$.",
  M("Pointwise multiplication commutes with applying the same record permutation to both operands, so the forward outputs can be multiplied in their retained order and that order passed directly to the opposite procedure.", "explicit",
    "Pointwise multiplication commutes with applying the same record"),
  [
    I("S2.4.1", "Expansion of finite sums gives $F_{\\boldsymbol t}^-(f*g)=M(F_{\\boldsymbol t}^-f)(F_{\\boldsymbol t}^-g)$, and since $F_{\\boldsymbol t}^+F_{\\boldsymbol t}^-=I/M$ it follows that $MF_{\\boldsymbol t}^+\\bigl((F_{\\boldsymbol t}^-f)(F_{\\boldsymbol t}^-g)/r\\bigr)=(f*g)/(rM)$.",
      "F_{\\boldsymbol t}^-(f*g)"),
    I("S2.4.2", "Since $LB$ permutes records and leaves polynomial suffixes intact, $P(LBu,LBv)=LBP(u,v)$ for the pointwise product $P(u,v)=uv/r$, so $\\mathcal T_+=F_{\\boldsymbol t}^+B^{-1}L^{-1}$ applied to the product of the stored forward outputs equals $F_{\\boldsymbol t}^+P(F_{\\boldsymbol t}^-f,F_{\\boldsymbol t}^-g)$.",
      "Since $LB$ permutes records and leaves their polynomial suffixes"),
    I("S2.4.3", "Each forward output has error less than $\\delta=\\sqrt2\\,\\ell\\,2^{-p}$ and $P$ is a bilinear contraction on disk arrays, so $\\|P(\\widetilde u,\\widetilde v)-P(u,v)\\|_\\infty<2\\delta$.",
      "\\|P(\\widetilde u,\\widetilde v)-P(u,v)\\|_\\infty"),
    I("S2.4.4", "The product's truncation adds $\\eta=\\sqrt2\\,2^{-p}$, so $\\|\\widetilde z-z_*\\|_\\infty<2\\delta+\\eta$.",
      "$\\|\\widetilde z-z_*\\|_\\infty<2\\delta+\\eta$."),
    I("S2.4.5", "The opposite transform is a contraction with its own error $\\delta$, so $\\|\\widetilde h-\\mathcal T_+z_*\\|_\\infty<3\\delta+\\eta$.",
      "<3\\delta+\\eta."),
    I("S2.4.6", "The final exact multiplication by $M$ gives error less than $M(3\\delta+\\eta)=\\sqrt2\\,M(3\\ell+1)2^{-p}$.",
      "$M(3\\delta+\\eta)=\\sqrt2\\,M(3\\ell+1)2^{-p}$, as claimed."),
    I("S2.4.7", "There are $M=T/r$ pointwise polynomial products of total cost $O(Tp\\log(rp))$ and the three transforms cost as stated, so the total is the claimed bound.",
      "There are $M=T/r$ pointwise polynomial products.  Their total cost"),
  ],
  frm=["synthetic-transform", "signed-packing", "fast-butterfly-layer", "address-chunk-interchange", "fixed-point-truncation", "polynomial-ring-mod", "cyclic-convolution", "multidimensional-dft", "dft"],
  salient="The error constant $3\\ell+1$ counts exactly three transform errors and one product truncation, because contractions never amplify the accumulated error; the exact scale $M=T/r$ is a power of two and keeps the $p$-bit grid.",
  adds=[B("M", "record count", "The number $T/r$ of polynomial records, the product of the first $d-1$ axis lengths.", "count"),
        B("T", "array size", "The total number $rM$ of complex scalars, equal to the product of all axis lengths.", "count"),
        B("r", "ring degree", "The number of complex coefficients of each polynomial record in $\\mathbb C[y]/(y^r+1)$; a power of two.", "count"),
        B("p", "precision", "The number of fractional bits of the fixed-point grid $2^{-p}\\mathbb Z[i]$.", "count")])

